;;; `std.algebra/functional` — ∀a. |{b : R(a,b)}| ≤ 1: one `a` with two
;;; distinct images is ⊥.
;;;
;;; ── Why this file is the census's finding, not just its coverage ───────────
;;;
;;; `functional` fires in the corpus and has **never once been productive**:
;;; 2 000 redundant firings, no productive ones, and outside
;;; `examples/ein-bugs/zebra2-bad.ein` it is never activated at all
;;; (docs/history/m1c_external_validation/stdlib_census.md §5).
;;; It is dominated twice over: `std.bijection`'s `functional-negative` at 240
;;; excludes every other partner the moment a positive lands, and the totality
;;; checks at 110 reach `(false)` first when a contradiction does occur. So in
;;; a puzzle this rule is never the first to refute, and a fixture that merely
;;; made it fire alongside the others would record that rather than test it.
;;;
;;; What this file does instead is put it somewhere nothing else can reach:
;;; **`functional` alone**, no bijection stack, no negative completion, no
;;; totality. Then the ⊥ is its and only its, and the `(neq ?b ?c)` guard —
;;; the same shape as the `disjunctive-prune` guard that was wrong for a year
;;; — has a test in the direction that finds a bug. The other direction, an
;;; edge matched against itself, is `08_checks_satisfied.ein`'s `pick`.

(import std.algebra :symbols (functional))

(relation assigned Task Worker :why "{?1} is assigned to {?2}")

(functional assigned)

(assigned Build Ann)
(assigned Build Bob)   ; ⊥ — one task, two distinct workers

(query
  :goal (assigned ?task ?who)
  :no-hypothesis (assigned)
  :expect (false))
